The '''Okaiken Socialist Party''' ({{Nihongo|岡井県社会党|Okaiken Shakaitō|}}), commonly known as the '''OSP''' ({{lang|ja|社会党}}, ''Shakaitō''), is a [[left-wing]] [[democratic socialist]] political party in [[Okaiken]].

The party has a tumultuous and divided history. It was founded in 1955 as the main opposition to the post-war conservative government. It briefly and disastrously held power from 1966 to 1968 under [[Genzui Kusaka]], whose government attempted to abolish parliament, leading to the [[Quiet Revolution]] and the party being banned for 20 years.

After being re-established in 1988, the OSP existed as a minor party for decades. It suffered a near-total collapse in 2021, being reduced to just two seats. The party saw a historic resurgence in the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]] under the populist leadership of [[Rento Nakayama]]. It currently serves as the junior coalition partner in a PPO-led government.

==History==

===Founding and early opposition (1955–1966)===
The Okaiken Socialist Party was formed on October 19, 1955, as a reunification of various socialist groups. Under its founding leader, '''Joji Kawaguchi''', the OSP quickly established itself as the "second most powerful political force" and the primary opposition to the conservative [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|Progressive Party (PPO)]] and Democratic Party governments. Throughout the late 1950s and early 1960s, the OSP built its base among trade unions and urban laborers, criticizing the Shaydian-influenced policies of the government. After failing to achieve a breakthrough in the 1964 election, Kawaguchi resigned.

===Kusaka Government and Quiet Revolution (1966–1968)===
In the mid-1960s, Okaiken faced a minor economic recession and high inflation, leading to the [[Okami Stock Exchange]] crash on October 15, 1967. This economic anxiety, combined with allegations of "vote-rigging," propelled the OSP under its new, charismatic leader, '''[[Genzui Kusaka]]''', to a surprise victory in the 1966 general election.

Once in power, the Kusaka government proved to be radically authoritarian. Citing "concerns of vote rigging," the Kusaka government drafted legislation that would abolish the elected Diet and replace it with a "non-voting system where a select committee can select nominees from other parties." This move was seen by the public as a naked power grab.

The proposed legislation was met with a furious backlash. The other parties, including the PPO, boycotted the Diet, effectively shutting down the government. Mass protests erupted in Okaiken's major cities, culminating in riots in 1968. This period of civil unrest became known as the [[Quiet Revolution]]. On August 12, 1968, the OSP government was forcibly unseated.

===Ban and "Winter Period" (1968–1988)===
As a direct result of its attempt to dismantle the republic, the Okaiken Socialist Party was formally dissolved by the Diet in late 1968 and banned from participating in national politics. The party's assets were seized, and its leaders were barred from holding public office.

For twenty years, the party existed only as a fragmented, extra-parliamentary "underground" movement, a time often referred to by party members as the "Winter Period."

===Re-establishment and minor party era (1988–2021)===
The ban on the OSP was lifted in 1988. The party was re-established by its new leader, '''Kenjiro Sano''', but as a shadow of its former self, its brand deeply tainted by the legacy of Genzui Kusaka. Sano led the party to a minor 12-seat victory in 1990 and joined the broad anti-LDP coalition of [[Shigehara Honda]]. After losing seats in 1993, Sano resigned.

Under his successor, '''Tatsuo Akamatsu''', the party saw modest growth in the late 1990s but remained a marginal force. Akamatsu's tenure (1995-2003) is remembered as a period of ideological stagnation, and he resigned after a poor showing in the 2003 election.

In 2006, the party elected '''[[Oizumi Yu]]''' as its new leader. Oizumi began a long-term project of slowly rebuilding the party's credibility. He led the OSP for 15 years, seeing gradual success that peaked in the [[Okaiken general election, 2016|2016 election]], where the party won 46 seats. This success made the OSP a key partner in the "left-wing" coalition government led by [[Sho Aoyagi]] of the [[Sanese Communist Party|Communist Party (SCP)]].

This coalition proved fatal for the OSP. Voters who had supported the party's moderate socialism felt betrayed by the Aoyagi government's scandals and high inflation, and particularly by Aoyagi's 2021 admission that he had broken a key pacifist campaign promise and increased defense spending.

This betrayal led to the party's annihilation in the [[Okaiken general election, 2021|2021 election]]. The OSP's vote collapsed, and the party was reduced from 46 seats to just 2. Oizumi Yu lost his own seat and resigned as party leader in disgrace. The party's two remaining Diet members selected '''Koga Mitsuo''' as a caretaker leader.

===2025 resurgence and "Nakayama-mania"===
The near-extinct party was considered politically irrelevant until May 2024, when the OSP leadership election was won by '''[[Rento Nakayama]]''', a 32-year-old former bodybuilder, actor, and social media influencer, who defeated the interim leader Koga Mitsuo.

Nakayama immediately transformed the party, abandoning its old, rigid doctrine in favor of a modern, populist, and [[democratic socialist]] platform. He ran a viral campaign almost entirely on social media, creating a phenomenon known as "Nakayama-mania" that attracted thousands of young, previously disengaged voters.

In the [[Okaiken general election, 2025|2025 general election]], the OSP achieved the greatest single-election comeback in Okaiken history, surging from 2 seats to 50. The result made the OSP the "kingmaker" in the new hung parliament. Nakayama agreed to form a coalition with [[Honda Shigetsuma]]'s PPO, becoming the junior partner in government.

==Ideology and Factions==
The OSP has been defined by two distinct, opposing ideologies.

* '''Old OSP (1955–1968)''': The party of [[Genzui Kusaka]] was a hard-line, [[statism|statist]] socialist party. It was deeply authoritarian and anti-democratic, believing that the party, not the people, was the correct vehicle for social change, which led to its attempt to abolish parliament.
* '''New OSP (2024–Present)''': The party of [[Rento Nakayama]] is a [[democratic socialist]] and populist party. It is staunchly [[anti-austerity]] and [[non-interventionism|non-interventionist]]. It advocates for abolishing university tuition, massive public investment in healthcare (especially mental health), and cutting the defense budget to fund social programs.

